# ============================================================
# ERP ENVIRONMENT — the ERP's own database, and nothing else
# ============================================================
#
# ERP_DATABASE_URL, deliberately NOT DATABASE_URL.
#
# DATABASE_URL on a developer's machine points at the Casa De Aurum
# WEBSITE's database. If the ERP read that name too, running a
# migration with the website's environment loaded in the same shell
# would try to migrate the website's production database with the
# ERP's schema. Under its own name that cannot happen by accident.
#
# Copy this file to .env and fill it in. Never commit .env.

ERP_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/devyora_erp"

# Only needed where the main URL goes through a connection pooler
# (pgbouncer in transaction mode, e.g. Supabase port 6543). The
# migration engine needs session-level advisory locks and prepared
# statements that transaction pooling does not provide, so it uses
# this direct connection instead. Leave unset where there is no
# pooler; the CLI then uses ERP_DATABASE_URL.
# ERP_DIRECT_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/devyora_erp"

# ============================================================
# CASA INTEGRATION — Step 9, the CASA backend reading this ERP
# ============================================================
#
# Shared secret for GET /api/v1/integrations/casa/catalog-feed,
# mirrored from CASA's own x-erp-key/ERP_EXPORT_API_KEY pattern
# (the opposite direction). CASA's backend sends this value in an
# x-casa-key header; server/middleware/casaAuth.mjs checks it on
# every request and fails closed if this is unset -- the route
# then refuses ALL callers, not just unauthenticated ones.
#
# Deliberately a separate secret from any other internal key.
# Leave unset in any environment the CASA backend has no business
# calling into.
CASA_INTEGRATION_API_KEY=
